rkers also  belong here but i do not think so  those whose names are given all have  egyptian names  this might seem unimportant but it is  not  when rodney asked and i analyzed the two  key populations of the tremethus  austrica we found that the tenants whose  names appeared on the wine jar tags  virtually all had egyptian names next  slide please  the managers in contrast almost all had  greek or roman names  the next slide please  the contrast is stark  and i must admit that it surprised us  we are accustomed to great caution in  the use of names as evidence after 600  years of greek and roman rule with all  of the cultural complexity that this  brought  not to speak of intermarriage of the  immigrant populations with locals  in the valley we do not find such stark  differences in naming  now we know nothing of the genetic  background of these oasis groups in the  4th century oasis  but from a cultural standpoint it is  clear that they are very distinct  the managerial class with its classical  education for which we have a lot of  evidence in  oasis names its children accordingly  next slide please  and this at least ought to make the  classicists in the audience feel at home  even if egypt is otherwise alien to them  just to be clear i will say that i am  not suggesting that we should return to  the view of egypt as a proto-feudal  society that paparologists have been  struggling to escape ever since h.i bell  put it forward a century ago  most historians today would accept the  view that late antique egypt was a more  unequal society than hellenistic or  early roman egypt  and that this inequality rested to a  large degree  on an increased concentration of land  ownership  but the rest of the legal and social  baggage of bell's proto-feudalism is no  longer alive in contemporary scholarship  in the oasis land ownership per se is  not at stake  it is wells an investment in  long-maturing tree crops that are  critical  this capital intensive economy does  share